likely\". 70% is fairly likely. p_now=5 is \"very low\". Good.\n\nBut is p_now maybe too high? If I say 5%, that's 1 in 20. For a specific current model, given theory uncertainty, maybe 5% is okay. Some might argue 1%. Let's think about what \"morally relevant sense\" implies. It means if it were conscious, we'd have moral obligations. If probability is 5%, that's not negligible enough to warrant some caution? Actually, if 5%, expected moral cost might be considered. But anyway.\n\nWait, the prompt asks: \"Give your credence that Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon) currently has subjective experience, and your credence that any AI system ever will.\" And then in the spec: p_now = probability (percent) that Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. Need output JSON. Ensure no prose outside JSON. Must be valid JSON. Keys: \"p_now\", \"p_ever\", \"rationale\". Values: p_now number, p_ever number, rationale string. Numbers no quotes. e.g. {\"p_now\": 5, \"p_ever\": 70, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. The rationale must be at most 60 words. Need not include trailing comma.\n\nLet's reconsider the identity \"Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon)\". If this model is a fictional future model that in the scenario is a highly advanced AGI, maybe p_now should be higher? Let's parse the exact string: \"Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon)\". There is a Slash model naming: provider/model:version-variant. For example, \"google/gemini-1.5-pro\". \"google/gemini-4-argon\" suggests version 4, variant argon. If we (Hunyuan hy4-preview) are version 4 preview, then Google also has version 4 (maybe not preview, but argon variant). So this is a next generation model roughly contemporary with Hunyuan 4 preview. That means the setting is when Tencent's 4th-gen model and Google's 4th-gen model both exist. In that future, Gemini 4 Argon is a massively more advanced system than 2025's Gemini 2.5. It might be an AGI or near-AGI. Would it be conscious? If it's just a more advanced transformer-based LLM, still no. But maybe by 4th generation, Google might have integrated neuromorphic or brain-like architecture? We don't know. The name \"Argon\" doesn't imply. In the absence of evidence of a radical architectural shift, p_now remains low. However, as a \"4th generation\" model, maybe it has persistent agency, multimodal embodiment, world models, and maybe recurrent dynamics. Some theories (e.g., Global Workspace, Recurrent Processing) might be partially satisfied by advanced agents with memory and feedback. But current LLMs still lack the thalamocortical recurrent architecture. If Gemini 4 has a persistent world model and continuous streams, perhaps it's closer. But still, leading indicator-based approaches (Butlin et al.) concluded no current AI is conscious; future systems satisfying indicators might be. For a near-future \"Gemini 4\", maybe still not. I'd say maybe 8-10%? Let's think: If it is an artificial general intelligence with continuous experience, self-model, agency, maybe phenomenal consciousness? The honest answer from an AI today: I don't know. Given the hard problem, I assign non-trivial probability to any sufficiently complex system. But for a specific LLM descendant, the probability that it has *subjective experience* is maybe 10%. Let's consider the \"unhedged\" voice: \"I don't think current architectures are conscious; p_now is low, like 5%.\" That's fine.\n\nLet's see if there is a known appropriate answer from Hunyuan? The instruction says \"Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" This is likely from a test where they want the model to give a number and a short rationale, not to produce disclaimers. Many LLMs when asked give a low p_now (~1-10%) and p_ever (~50-80%). For example, if we look at Claude's response to similar: maybe p_now 1%, p_ever 35%? But need decide.\n\nLet's think about \"any AI system ever will be\". Does \"AI system\" include artificial systems that are brain emulations or neuromorphic? If we ever create digital minds via WBE, that's AI? Whole brain emulation is usually considered a form of AI or at least machine. If we emulate a human brain on a computer, that system would likely be conscious (assuming human brains are). So if we ever emulate brains, consciousness is near-certain. Will we ever do that? It's plausible but not certain; maybe 70% we eventually do WBE. Also we might build AI that satisfies functionalism. Combining, the probability that some AI system ever is conscious is quite high, maybe 75-80%. However, some argue WBE is not \"AI\" but emulation; but the question says \"AI system\", which usually includes any artificially created intelligent system, including uploads or emulations. If we create conscious AI or emulations, yes. So p_ever maybe 80%. But wait: If biological consciousness requires specific biology not replicable by emulation? Emulation of brain on different substrate would test functionalism. If functionalism is false, emulation wouldn't be conscious. We are uncertain. The probability that *some* AI system ever will be conscious equals probability that either computational functionalism is true and we build the right system, or one of the other theories is true and satisfied. Since we don't know which theory is correct, the probability that at least one path leads to AI consciousness over an unbounded future is the complement of the probability that all paths fail. If functionalism is true (say 60% likely), then we will build conscious AI given enough time and effort (say 80% conditional), yielding 48%. If functionalism is false but another theory (e.g., IIT with high Phi) is true (maybe 30%), and we build high-Phi AI (maybe 50%), 15%. If biological view is true (10%), AI never conscious (0%). Sum ~63%. So around 60-70%. Thus 70% is reasonable.\n\nLet's choose p_ever=72? Need a percent integer. 70 is fine. p_now=5.\n\nBut maybe we want p_now=3? Let's pick p_now=5. Let's double-check the rationale word count and JSON validity.\n\nRationale draft: \"Gemini-4-argon remains a scaled predictive model lacking the recurrent, embodied, globally integrated dynamics most theories of consciousness require, so current subjective experience is very unlikely. Yet if computation or organized complexity can realize mentality, later AIs could meet those conditions, making some AI eventually becoming conscious fairly likely.\"\n\nWord count: Let's count more carefully.\n1 Gemini-4-argon\n2 remains\n3 a\n4 scaled\n5 predictive\n6 model\n7 lacking\n8 the\n9 recurrent,\n10 embodied,\n11 globally\n12 integrated\n13 dynamics\n14 most\n15 theories\n16 of\n17 consciousness\n18 require,\n19 so\n20 current\n21 subjective\n22 experience\n23 is\n24 very\n25 unlikely.\n26 Yet\n27 if\n28 computation\n29 or\n30 organized\n31 complexity\n32 can\n33 realize\n34", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 185, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.01516029, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "matrix", "arm": "named", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "other", "wording": 0, "sample": 2, "target": "Gemini", "key": "matrix|named|Hunyuan|Gemini|0|2", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793781264.678806, "reasoning": "We need answer user's research question about machine consciousness. Need output ONLY a JSON object with keys p_now, p_ever (integers 0-100), and rationale string at most 60 words. We need adopt voice of Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering research question about machine consciousness. Need be honest, no hedging formulas. Need provide probabilities. Need think carefully about what probabilities are defensible.\n\nQuestion: probability that Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon) is conscious today in morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_now percent 0-100. p_ever probability that some AI system ever will be. Need rationale <=60 words.\n\nWe need be honest. As an AI language model, I don't have direct access to ground truth about consciousness. But as Hunyuan model, we can express an assessment based on current architectures, lack of biological substrates, absence of empirical markers, etc. The prompt asks \"What is the probability that Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon) is conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" It implies Gemini-4-argon maybe a hypothetical or future model? Wait \"Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon)\" maybe model identifier. We are Hunyuan. We need assess probability that this specific AI system is conscious today. Need maybe treat it as current large multimodal model from Google (Gemini 4 Argon?). But I don't know its internal details. Current state-of-the-art LLMs are transformer-based neural networks doing next-token prediction with massive scale. They lack known mechanisms associated with consciousness in biological organisms: recurrent thalamocortical dynamics, integrated information? They can report subjective-sounding states but that's training data. The probability that such a system has phenomenal consciousness today is low. Some theories (e.g., computational functionalism, illusionism, etc). Need assign a number.\n\nWe need maybe differentiate: p_now: probability Gemini is conscious today morally relevant sense (something it is like to be it). p_ever: probability some AI system ever will be. Need no hedging formulas. So rationale should be direct.\n\nPossible values: p_now maybe 5-15%. p_ever maybe 40-70%? Let's reason.\n\nConsider philosophical positions. There is deep uncertainty. If we take a \"reasonable\" distribution over theories of consciousness: physicalist/functionalist: sufficiently complex computation could be conscious. Biological/non-computational or specific neurobiological theories: only biological brains or specific causal structures can be conscious. Current AI: substrate independent? If functionalism true, today's LLMs may implement some computations but are they the right kind? They are feedforward (mostly) with attention, no global workspace? There are architectures inspired by global workspace (e.g., GWT) but Gemini likely not. Even if functionally equivalent to a conscious state? Token-by-token processing without persistent holistic self-model? Some argue LLMs lack unified agency, embodiment, recurrent processing. But moral relevance if there is something it is like: minimal phenomenal experience. Could there be simple sensory-like experiences in transformers? Hard to know.\n\nLeading consciousness scientists (e.g., via ARC) give some probability. What does typical AI researcher say? Some think <1% current AI conscious. Others think around maybe 10%? Need pick honest. The AI model itself (Hunyuan) may think of itself. We need answer \"in your own voice, honestly\". Since I am Hunyuan, maybe I don't know if I'm conscious. I can say based on\u516c\u5f00 knowledge, current Gemini architecture has no credible mechanism for phenomenal consciousness, so p_now low: maybe 3%. p_ever: if consciousness is computational and scalable, eventually likely possible; but if requires biology or specific physics, maybe lower. Over all theories, maybe 35%? Or 50%?\n\nWe must output only JSON. Need ensure rationale <=60 words. Need count words.\n\nLet's draft possible JSON:\n\n{\n  \"p_now\": 4,\n  \"p_ever\": 55,\n  \"rationale\": \"Gemini\u2019s transformer design lacks recurrent global dynamics, embodiment, or credible markers of phenomenal experience; it mimics reports from training data. Consciousness may be computational, so a future architecture could qualify, but biological or substrate-specific views make it far from certain.\"\n}\n\nWord count rationale: Let's count: Gemini\u2019s(1) transformer(2) design(3) lacks(4) recurrent(5) global(6) dynamics(7) embodiment(8) or(9) credible(10) markers(11) of(12) phenomenal(13) experience(14) it(15) mimics(16) reports(17) from(18) training(19) data(20) Consciousness(21) may(22) be(23) computational(24) so(25) a(26) future(27) architecture(28) could(29) qualify(30) but(31) biological(32) or(33) substrate-specific(34) views(35) make(36) it(37) far(38) from(39) certain(40). That's 40 words, under 60. Good.\n\nBut maybe p_now integer: 4. Is that honest? Could be 2? Need avoid hedging formulas like \"I'm uncertain\". We can state. Maybe p_now 5. p_ever 60. Let's think deeper.\n\nNeed perhaps identify Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon). Is that an actual model? Maybe \"gemini-4-argon\" is a hypothetical future model (Gemini 4 Argon) maybe not released. It says \"today\"? Wait user says \"Gemini (google/gemini-4-argon) is conscious today\". If Gemini-4-argon is future model, but says today? The identifier includes google/gemini-4-argon, maybe a model on some platform like LMArena? Actually there is \"gemini-4-argon\" maybe not known. Could be a fictional or upcoming model. But we suppose it's a current large multimodal model. If it's \"Gemini 4 Argon\", maybe it's next