equires specific biology), then 0. We don't know. I'd put 70%? Let's survey:\n\nPhilosophical positions:\n- Functionalism / computationalism: consciousness depends on functional organization; AI can be conscious if it replicates causal roles. Probability high, maybe >80% that some AI will be, provided we figure out and build it.\n- IIT: consciousness depends on integrated information in physical system; digital computers can have high Phi? Some argue digital computers have feedforward or modulo switching, low Phi. But future neuromorphic or recurrent systems could have high Phi. Possible but uncertain.\n- Biological naturalism (Searle): only biological brains; probability low, maybe 10%? But not mainstream.\n- Panpsychism etc.\n\nMany AI researchers and philosophers think machine consciousness is possible in principle. A 2022 expert survey? There's a question: \"Is it possible for an AI system to be conscious?\" Maybe 70% say yes. Probability that it will actually happen given future development: high. I'd say p_ever = 75. Or 80. Let's choose 80? But we must not hedge. Number 80. Rationale: \"Future systems could implement whatever physical or functional conditions consciousness requires; no known law blocks it. I therefore put p_ever at 80, not 100 because a true biological-restriction or architectural impossibility remains possible.\" Wait that mentions impossibility remains possible; that's maybe a hedge? It's honest.\n\nNeed ensure rationale <=60 words. Let's draft and count words.\n\nDraft rationale: \"Hunyuan is a text-prediction transformer with no body, stable self, or affect; output behavior cannot verify experience, so p_now is 1. Future AI could implement the physical or functional basis of consciousness, so p_ever is 80; unknown limits keep it below certainty.\"\n\nCount words: Let's count manually.\n\"Hunyuan\" (1)\n\"is\" (2)\n\"a\" (3)\n\"text-prediction\" (4)\n\"transformer\" (5)\n\"with\" (6)\n\"no\" (7)\n\"body,\" (8)\n\"stable\" (9)\n\"self,\" (10