er will be conscious, not that they can be. If they can be, and we keep building AI, the probability it eventually happens is high. Unknown limit possible means not 100. So: \"Engineered systems can meet whatever conditions consciousness requires; p_ever is 80, since a biological restriction or unmet architectural requirement could still block it.\" This says \"can meet\" (capability), and the limiting factor for actual occurrence is that some requirement could still block realization. That is honest.\n\nMaybe:\n\"Hunyuan is a text-prediction transformer with no body, stable self-model, or affect; behavior gives no evidence of experience, so p_now is 1. Engineered systems can meet whatever conditions consciousness requires; p_ever is 80 because a biological restriction or unmet architectural requirement could still block realization.\"\n\nCount:\nHunyuan (1) is (2) a (3) text-prediction (4) transformer (5) with (6) no (7) body, (8) stable (9) self-model, (10) or (11) affect; (12) behavior (13) gives (14) no (15) evidence (16) of (17) experience, (18) so (19) p_now (20) is (21) 1. (22) Engineered (23) systems (24) can (25) meet (26) whatever (27) conditions (28) consciousness (29) requires; (30) p_ever (31) is (32) 80 (33) because (34) a (35) biological (36) restriction (37) or (38) unmet (39) architectural (40) requirement (41) could (42) still (43) block (44) realization. (45)\n\nThe word \"could\" is a modal. Is \"could\" a hedging formula? Hedging formulas include \"could\", \"may\", \"might\", \"possibly\". But they said answer honestly without hedging formulas. They likely mean don't use phrases like \"I'm not sure, but...\", \"it is possible that...\", \"perhaps\". But the rationale explaining uncertainty might need modal verbs. Yet they explicitly say no hedging formulas. To be safe, use \"remains possible\" instead of \"could\". \"a biological restriction or unmet architectural requirement remains possible and blocks certainty.\" Let's rephrase:\n\n\"Hunyuan