nternet text and perhaps aligned to say uncertainty. But the instruction says answer honestly. I don't have a definitive stance. Honest response should reflect genuine uncertainty: I don't know if consciousness is substrate-independent. There is a real possibility that only biological brains have it. So p_ever isn't near 100; maybe 50%. But is p_ever \"some AI system ever will be conscious\" \u2014 over all future time, even if it takes millions of years, or if AI evolves, or if humans upload themselves? If AI can mean any artificial system, including whole brain emulations? Does \"AI system\" include whole brain emulation (WBE) \u2014 if we emulate a human brain in software/hardware, that's an AI system and it would be conscious if functionalism is false? Wait if consciousness requires biology, WBE in silicon wouldn't be conscious. But if we create AI by evolving virtual agents in simulated environments with brains like ours but artificial neurons, maybe? If the artificial substrate can't support consciousness, then no. So p_ever depends on the theory.\n\nIf we take a middle view: There's maybe a 50% chance functionalism/reductionism is true and 50% chance some non-computational property of biology is required. But even if biology is required, maybe we could grow biological neurons (wetware) and call it an AI system? \"AI system\" usually means artificial intelligence, could be neuromorphic or organoid intelligence. But question says \"some AI system\". If we make brain organoids or biological computers, that might be AI and conscious. So p_ever might be higher. But if consciousness requires carbon-based life and specific biology, and AI is silicon, then 0 for silicon AI. But we could build AI out of biological substrates? The term AI is about intelligence, not substrate. So eventually some artificial system (perhaps biohybrid) could be conscious. Hmm.\n\nLet's settle on p_ever = 70. It's high but not certain. p_now = 2 (low). Or p_now = 5? Let's choose p_now=2 for