e are obvious technical barriers to building conscious AI systems.\" That means p_ever is high, maybe 70-90%. And p_now maybe 1%? Because they say \"no current AI systems are conscious\" as a tentative conclusion. If they are 90% confident? The paper says: \"We reach the tentative conclusion that no current AI systems are conscious, but we do not think there are obvious technical barriers.\" So maybe p_now ~ 5% (not 1% because tentative). Let's quote: \"We conclude that, according to many of the most influential theories of consciousness, there are no current AI systems that are conscious. But there are variants...\" Hmm.\n\nLet's check memory: The report by Butlin, Long, Elmoznino, et al. (2023) \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\". They evaluate theories: Recurrent Processing Theory, Global Workspace Theory, Higher-Order Theories, Attention Schema Theory, Predictive Processing, and also \"Agency and Embodied\". They apply 14 indicators. They find that \"no current AI systems are conscious\", but several indicators are satisfied by some systems (e.g., Transformers have some global workspace-ish features? Actually they say \"Transformer-based LLMs have some features loosely analogous to a global workspace\"). They say \"our assessment is that no current AI systems are conscious, but this conclusion is uncertain\". They also say \"many indicators could in principle be satisfied by AI systems\". So p_now is low but not zero. They did not give probability. But if they say \"no current AI systems are conscious\", maybe median credence ~ 80% no consciousness => 20% yes? That seems high. But \"", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 198, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015019068, "finish": "length"}
