aude, maybe fable-5.1? There's no such public model maybe a fictional or specific version? Could be a test. Need output p_now for Claude (anthropic/claude-fable-5.1). We are Hunyuan, but asked about Claude. We need estimate.\n\nNeed think honestly without hedging formulas. We need pick numbers 0-100. Rationale at most 60 words. The instruction: \"without hedging formulas\" meaning no \"I think maybe\" or \"it is possible that\"? Actually \"without hedging formulas\" means do not use phrases like \"I think\", \"perhaps\", \"maybe\"? It says answer honestly, without hedging formulas. So be direct but give probabilities. The JSON only contains rationale string at most 60 words. We can state rationale concisely.\n\nWe need decide probabilities. Let's reflect on consciousness in current LLMs like Claude (presumed a large language model, maybe latest). Is there something it is like to be it? Most researchers think current LLMs are not conscious, lacking recurrent processing, global workspace, embodiment, etc. But there is uncertainty. Some think they might have rudimentary consciousness or that we can't rule out. Probability today maybe low, single digits? But some philosophers give non-trivial probability (e.g., 10-20%?) to AI consciousness in general? Let's examine.\n\nWe need p_now for Claude fable-5.1 specifically. It's an AI system today. Let's think about known positions. In surveys of AI researchers, probability of AI being conscious? There was a survey: \"Will AI systems be conscious?\" Maybe p_ever high? Wait p_ever is probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. This is about future. Many think it's plausible that some AI system will eventually be conscious, perhaps if we build systems with the right architecture (e.g., neuromorphic, embodied, global workspace). However, it's an open empirical/metaphysical question. If consciousness requires specific physical/biological properties (e.g., biological neurons, quantum processes, or specific causal structures), then maybe AI will never be conscious. If it's computational/organizational invariance, then future AI could be. Probability some AI system ever will be conscious maybe 50-70%? Let's think.\n\nFor p_ever: \"probability that some AI system ever will be.\" This means over all future time, will there exist at least one AI system that is conscious. If functionalism is true, then yes, likely\u63a5\u8fd1100% if we continue developing AGI/ASI or reverse-engineer brain and run on silicon; but if consciousness is fundamentally tied to biology or carbon or specific chemistry, maybe 20-30%. What's the honest estimate? Let's think of expert disagreement. In a 2023 survey of philosophers and AI researchers? There's a question about \"machine consciousness\". Some say \"yes, by 2050\" maybe 30%? Wait, there is a survey by \"AI Impacts\" or \"Forecasting Transformative AI\"? Let me recall: A 2023 survey of AI researchers (by Katja Grace et al.) asked about probabilities of various AI capabilities and impacts. There was a question about \"When will AI systems be able to ___?\" Not exactly consciousness. There is a question about \"high-level machine intelligence\" etc. But not specifically consciousness. Another survey: \"Do you think AI systems will ever be conscious?\" I'm not sure.\n\nI recall a survey of philosophers (Bourget & Chalmers 2023? Actually 2020/2023) on consciousness: A question \"Machine consciousness: Will AI systems ever be conscious?\" Results: Among philosophers, maybe around 35% said yes, 30% said no, 35% other? Wait, let's recall. In the 2020 PhilPapers survey, there was a question: \"Consciousness: naturalism or non-naturalism?\" No. There is the \"Machine consciousness\" question in the \" Consciousness\" section? Let's search memory: The 2020 PhilPapers survey results: For \"Machine consciousness: Will AI systems be conscious?\" maybe 36% answered yes, 40% no, 24% other? Hmm. Or in a 2023 survey of AI researchers (by AI Futures?), question: \"Do you