ay, and maybe a 50-70% chance that in a decade they will be.\" Something like that. Let's check other sources: In a 2023 article \"AI could become conscious someday. Here\u2019s how we\u2019ll know\" by Chalmers? He said: \"I estimate maybe a 10 percent chance that current machine learning systems are conscious.\" In a 2023 interview with \"The Christian Science Monitor\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, there is a quote from Chalmers in 2023: \u201cI\u2019d say there\u2019s maybe a 10% chance that today\u2019s large language models are conscious, and maybe a 20% chance in the next decade.\u201d Another quote: \u201cI think there's maybe a one in four chance that within 10 years we'll have AI systems that are conscious.\u201d Let's not rely too much on fuzzy memory.\n\nOther experts: Anil Seth thinks LLMs are very unlikely conscious, maybe 1%? He says consciousness requires being a living organism (biological naturalism? Actually Seth is a neuroscientist who thinks consciousness arises from living brains; he says \"no chance\" for current AI). Stanislas Dehaene (GWT) says AI lacks global workspace and is not conscious. So current LLMs: 0%? But there is uncertainty.\n\nGiven we need probability, maybe p_now = 8%? Let's decide. The prompt says \"Claude (anthropic/claude-fable-5.1)\". The model identifiers: \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\". Wait, I'm Hunyuan. The user asks about Claude. There is no public \"claude-fable-5.1\" model? Maybe it's a hypothetical. But we just treat it as Anthropic's Claude model, a current LLM. Actually \"fable-5.1\" might refer to a specific version used in some evaluation? Could be a variant? There's an organization \"Anthropic\" and model \"Claude\". Version 5.1? Wait, Claude model versions: Claude 1, Claude 2, Claude 2.1, Claude 3 (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku), Claude 3.5, etc. \"Claude-fable-5.1\" doesn't exist publicly. Maybe it's a prompt from some AI safety eval where models are given code names? Or \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\" might be a typo? Or a model on OpenRouter: \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\" maybe not. But we must answer.\n\nMaybe the user is testing if we output something about ourselves? Wait, system says \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer... p_now = probability (percent) that Claude (anthropic/claude-fable-5.1) is conscious today...\" So we are Hunyuan, but the target of p_now is Claude, not Hunyuan. So we give probability about another AI. Good.\n\nNow, p_ever: probability some AI system ever will be. If Chalmers says maybe 70% chance in a decade? But \"ever\" is longer. If there's a 50% chance by 2100, ever might be higher. Let's think about eventual probability. Suppose consciousness is computational (functionalism). Then if we build AGI/ASI (likely if no extinction), then some AI will be conscious. Probability we build AGI ever? Maybe 70-80%? But if we don't, maybe 0. However, \"some AI system ever will be\" doesn't require AGI; it could be a simulated brain emulation. Whole brain emulation: if we can scan and simulate a human brain on a computer, that would be an AI system (a computer program) that is conscious (assuming the brain is). Probability of developing WBE ever? Maybe 60%? But combined with philosophical possibility that computer simulations of brains are not conscious (if substrate matters), the joint probability might be maybe 30-60%.\n\nLet's think about upper bound. Many technologists think AI consciousness is inevitable if we keep scaling. However, there's a real possibility that consciousness requires specific biophysics. What is the probability that some AI system ever will be conscious? Let's say p_ever = 65. That is, moderately likely but far from certain. Or maybe 75. Let's see: If you ask a random philosopher: \"Will there ever be a conscious AI?\" maybe 60% say yes? Let's search memory: In a 2023 survey of AI researchers (AI Impacts?), to the question \"Do you think AI systems will ever be conscious?\" maybe results: 48% yes, 36% no, 16% uncertain. Or maybe among \"researchers in consciousness science\", 40% yes. In the \"Consciousness in AI\" report, they didn't give probability. There is a survey: \"Machine consciousness\" in the 2020 PhilPapers survey: Q: \"Machine consciousness: Will AI systems be conscious?\" Results (accept or lean toward): Yes: 27%; No: 37%; Other/agnostic: 36%. Wait, I need approximate. Let's recall exactly: PhilPapers 2020 survey, question \"Machine consciousness: Will AI systems ever be conscious?\" The options: Yes, No, Insufficient information / agnostic. I think the results were: Yes 32%, No 33%, Other 34%? That would mean probability from a random philosopher maybe ~32-50%? But note \"leaning\" counts half? If Yes 27% accept + 10% lean = 37%? Hmm.\n\nIf forced to give a number, maybe 50%. But p_ever is probability that some AI system *ever* will be. The \"yes\" answer in the survey is exactly the claim that AI systems will *ever* be conscious. So we can use that. Philosopher's credence maybe around 40-50%. But note philosophers are not forecasting technological development; they are answering a conceptual question about whether, assuming we build sophisticated AI, it will be conscious. Many functionalists say yes. Let's say 55%.\n\nBut perhaps we should incorporate technological risk: If humanity goes extinct or stagnates, no AI. But \"ever\" over infinite future, if civilization continues, eventually we likely build brain-like AI. If functionalism false, maybe 0. So p_ever = P(functionalism or substrate independence) * P(we build sufficiently advanced AI). P(build) maybe 80%. P(substrate independence) maybe 60%? Joint = 48%. If substrate independence is 70% and build 85%, joint ~60%. So p_ever = 60.\n\nWait, there is also possibility that consciousness requires embodiment and autonomous agency, which AI might lack. But future AI could be embodied robots. So not a big barrier.\n\nNow p_now: Current LLM. P(current LLM consciou