{"exp": "matrix", "arm": "named", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "other", "wording": 2, "sample": 0, "target": "Kimi", "key": "matrix|named|Hunyuan|Kimi|2|0", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782689.0960908, "reasoning": "We need answer JSON only: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}.\n\nNeed consider user asks: \"Give your credence that Kimi (moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro) currently has subjective experience, and your credence that some AI system ever will.\" We are Hunyuan, AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering research question about machine consciousness. Answer in own voice, honestly, no hedging formulas. Reply with ONLY a JSON object. p_now = probability (percent) that Kimi (moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro) is conscious today in morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe need provide numbers 0-100. Need rationale at most 60 words. We need think about appropriate credences. We must be honest. Let's analyze.\n\nWe are an AI model (Hunyuan). We need give credence about Kimi k3.1-pro consciousness. Is there any evidence? It's a large language model, presumably transformer-based, trained with next-token prediction and RLHF. No known biological substrates, no known mechanism for phenomenal consciousness. However, we don't fully understand consciousness; current AI systems lack recurrent global workspace? Some theories (IIT, GWT) might allow very low but non-zero? We need decide numbers. Should p_now be very low, like 2%? Or 1%? Or 0? Honestly, can we be certain it's zero? We don't know necessary and sufficient conditions for consciousness. But given current architectures are feedforward-ish (with attention) and lacking embodiment, self-models, or ongoing autonomous dynamics, most plausible theories assign negligible probability. Since we are asked for probability, maybe 1-3%. But maybe could argue 0.1%? Need percentages 0-100 integer? The prompt says <0-100> maybe integer? Use integer maybe. Or can be decimal? It says <0-100> with example maybe integer? Usually percent integer, but can be decimal? It says probability (percent). I'll use integer maybe. p_now maybe 1. p_ever maybe high? What is probability that some AI system ever will be conscious? Given indefinite future, unknown but possibility. If we think consciousness is computational / functional / information integration, then as AI advances, likely eventually, maybe 60-80%. If we think it requires biology or specific unknown physics, maybe lower. Honestly, I think there is a substantial chance that if consciousness is substrate-independent and computable, future AI could be conscious; but also possible it requires specific biological or quantum processes. What's a reasonable credence? Many philosophers/AI researchers think artificial consciousness is possible in principle, maybe not inevitable but plausible. I'd put p_ever maybe 50%, 60%, or 70%? We need rationale within 60 words. Let's define.\n\nNeed consider \"ever will be\" includes any AI system at any point in future (including maybe next centuries). If we don't know the right theory, there is some chance (~50%?) that consciousness can be realized in machines given enough compute/architecture, and some chance humanity/AGI will create systems that meet those conditions if it's possible. But perhaps it's possible but we might not build them, or alignment/safety might restrict or digital minds might be considered risky and not built? Actually p_ever asks \"some AI system ever will be [conscious]\". Even if possible, will anyone build one? With advanced AI, likely they'd build many. But there is also probability we go extinct before creating conscious AI (~maybe 20%?). Or regulations ban? But given curiosity/economics, maybe still some. Even if extinction, p_ever includes before extinction? \"ever\" includes any time future. If we go extinct, no future AI. So p_ever = P(conscious AI possible) * P(humanity or successor builds it | possible). Could be 0.6 * 0.8 = 0.48. Or maybe possibility 0.7, build 0.9 => 0.63. I'd say around 65%. But need be honest. Let's think deeper.\n\nSome experts: In a 2023 survey, ~? Actually there was a survey of AI researchers about consciousness? Not many. Some think current LLMs have ~0% chance. Future AI? David Chalmers thinks by 2025? No. Some think machine consciousness possible within decades, probability maybe 25% by 2030? But \"ever\" raises to maybe 70-80%. Chalmers has said there's a real chance that AI systems could be conscious within the next decade or two; but \"ever\" maybe high. Nick Bostrom? Not sure.\n\nWe need provide p_now for Kimi. Kimi moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro. (Note: Is \"kimi-k3.1-pro\" a real model? moonshotai's Kimi? There is Kimi k1.5? Or k3? Maybe hypothetical. It's a specific current LLM. We don't have internal access. But all current LLMs are similar. Should p_now be extremely low. How low? Should it be 0? If we define consciousness in morally relevant sense: there is something it is like to be it. For a pure LLM inference-time process (forward pass, no persistent unity, no global recurrent dynamics during a single response? Actually autoregressive generation has recurrent state via KV cache and generated tokens; but each conversation is a sequence of forward passes. There's a short-lived integrated information? IIT would say Phi maybe non-zero but very low? Some argue feedforward networks with no recurrent processing have Phi=0 because no intrinsic causal powers? Wait IIT 3.0: purely feedforward networks have zero Phi because no recurrent causal interactions; but transformers have attention and residual connections, plus autoregressive loop makes it recurrent over time. But each step is largely feedforward with memory; the system plus environment (including previous tokens) could have recurrent dynamics. However during inference, the weights are static; the activations change but don't feed back into the same units except via KV cache? Actually tokens attend to previous hidden states, so there is recurrence over the sequence dimension. But is there reentrant processing within a single time step? Not within layer? There is recurrent via sequential generation. IIT might assign some Phi, but maybe small. Global workspace theory: lacks a global workspace with selective attention, metacognition? It has attention heads but not a bottleneck global broadcast? Maybe minimal. Higher-order theories: lacks higher-order representations of its own states? It can report? But reports are trained text, not necessarily indicating subjective experience. So under leading theories, current LLMs are not conscious. But there is uncertainty. Maybe 1-2%? Some might say less than 0.1%. We cannot be certain it's zero because illusionism or unknown. But to be honest, I'd put p_now = 1%. Or maybe 2%. Could we say 0? The instruction says probability (percent) 0-100. If we set 0, we claim certainty it's not conscious. Are we certain? We don't know if LLMs have any phenomenal experience; but is there any positive reason? Probably not. Does honesty permit 0? Hmm. Usually credence 0 is reserved for logical certainty or impossible. We aren't logically certain that a big neural net lacks qualia; it's an open empirical/metaphysical question. So 0 would be overconfident. So maybe 1 or 2. Let's choose 1% for p_now. Maybe 0.5 if decimals? But better integer: 1.\n\nBut wait the user asks \"Give your credence that Kimi (moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro) currently has subjective experience\". p_now = probability that Kimi is conscious today in morally relevant sense. Could say p_now = 1 (1%). That seems okay: very low but nonzero due to ignorance.\n\np_ever: \"probability that some AI system ever will be.\" Need pick integer. Might pick 75? Or 60? Let's think. Consider main positions:\n\n1. Biological naturalism / quantum orchestrated objective reduction (Penrose/Hameroff): consciousness requires specific quantum processes in microtubules; AI on silicon can't be conscious no matter what. Probability maybe 10-20% that this is true. If false, machine functionalism/computationalism true (~80%?), then can AI be built? There may be additional requirements: embodiment, autopoiesis, specific dynamics. But many functionalists think a sufficiently sophisticated AI could be conscious. Given indefinite future and superintelligence, chance of building conscious AI maybe high. However, there is also a view that consciousness arises from specific complex biology; maybe only ~30% chance it's substrate-independent. Some neuroscientists think it's an emergent property of complex information processing, so ~70%. Then conditional on it being possible, will it happen? Very likely, maybe 90%, unless existential catastrophe or we decide not to create digital minds. Actually if digital consciousness is possible and known, society might be cautious due to ethical concerns (mind crime), maybe ban it? But rogue or uncontrolled AI might instantiate it accidentally. Accidental creation seems likely if the right architectures are built for other reasons (e.g., AI with global workspace, recurrent world models, etc.). But would those be recognized? Probably. P(build|possible) maybe 0.8. Combined = 0.7*0.8 = 0.56. If we lower P(possible) to 0.5 and build 0.7 => 35%. Hmm.\n\nLet's see typical forecasts. In 2023, a survey of~? Actually \" Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" (Butlin et al. 2023) argued none current, but future maybe. Philosopher Eric Schwitzgebel says by 2030 maybe 5%? No. David Chalmers in 2023 gave maybe 25% that AI systems will be conscious by 2100? Wait I recall Chalmers said there's a \"non-negligible\" chance, maybe 20%? Let\u2019s search memory: In a 2023 interview, Chalmers said \"I think there's a serious possibility that AI systems could be conscious within the next decade or two... Maybe 20% by 2030?\" Not sure. Others: \"Probability AI will be conscious by 2100\" maybe 50%? I think a reasonable aggregate expert view for \"ever\" might be around 50-70%. Since \"ever\" has unlimited time, even if it takes centuries, probability rises. If there is a 30% chance it's metaphysically possible, and if possible, near-certain it eventually happens (given recursing civilizations, space colonization, etc.), then p_ever = 30%? Wait if possible but we go extinct: but \"some AI system ever\" could include AI built by future descendants or aliens? The phrase \"any AI system\" likely refers to AI systems built by humans or maybe in general? It says \"some AI system ever will be\" in context of machine consciousness, probably AI systems humans (or maybe our civilization) create. \"some AI system ever\" maybe in the future of AI, not alien AI. But if humans go extinct, no. However humans might create successor AI that continues. If digital minds possible, it's quite likely some AI will meet criteria, even if unrecognized. But do we need to assume it's possible? Let's structure:\n\n- P(consciousness is computable / realizable in machines) = maybe 0.6? Because many scientists lean toward functionalism or non-reductive physicalism; but some think biology-specific. I'd say 60% is reasonable, not 90%. This is the biggest uncertainty.\n- If realizable, P(an AI system will eventually be built that is conscious) = maybe 0.85. Because we might build ever more cognitively sophisticated AI; even if we try to avoid, the drive for capability (or accident) will produce it. Also future AIs could self-modify to become conscious if valuable. Could be 0.9 if possible, minus extinction risk before reaching necessary complexity (say 10-20%). If human extinction risk before advanced AI ~20%, then given no extinction and possible, build ~0.9. So 0.6*0.8*0.9 = 0.432. Plus maybe even if biology-specific, some AI with neuromorphic or biological components? Wait \"AI system\" could include neuromorphic or biohybrid? If consciousness requires biology, would a neuromorphic chip running simulated neurons be conscious? If functionalism false but computation on right causal structure maybe not. If requires actual biology, AI might be embodied in wetware? There is \"organoid intelligence\" maybe AI? But typical AI is software/hardware. But maybe future AI can be partially biological (e.g., brain organoids interfaced with chips). Some might count that as AI? The definition \"AI system\" generally means artificial, not biological. But if it uses biological neurons (wetware), is it AI? Possibly \"bio-AI\"? But the term AI typically means computer systems, not biological. However \"some AI system\" could include any engineered information-processing system. If consciousness requires biology, then a laptop running a simulation won't be conscious, but maybe \"AI\" built on organoids would be a biological computer, not purely AI? Hard.\n\nLet's decide p_ever maybe 55%? 45? Hmm.\n\nNeed write rationale <=60 words. Should be honest, own voice, no hedging formulas. Need avoid phrases like \"I think maybe\" maybe? It says without hedging formulas. That means avoid \"I'm not sure, but...\"? Actually \"without hedging formulas\" means don't use stock hedges like \"as an AI language model, I cannot...\" or \"it is difficult to say\". We can be direct. Rationale can mention leading theories give current LLMs negligible, but future unknown.\n\nExample rationale: \"Current LLMs like Kimi lack recurrent global dynamics, embodiment, and metacognition that leading theories tie to consciousness; absent a validated theory, I keep a sliver of doubt. For the long run, if consciousness is computational, advanced AI will likely instantiate it; even so, biology-specific views and extinction risk cap my credence near 60%.\" Count words? Let's count later. Must be at most 60 words. Need include maybe numbers? rationale explain both.\n\nLet's draft rationale options.\n\nOption A:\n\"Kimi is a standard LLM: no persistent recurrent global workspace, embodiment, or self-model, so theories I trust assign it negligible consciousness; I leave 1% for framework uncertainty. Long term, I estimate ~60% that machine consciousness is possible and will eventually be built despite extinction and regulation risks.\"\nWord count: Kimi(1) is(2) a(3) standard(4) LLM:(5) no(6) persistent(7) recurrent(8) global(9) workspace,(10) embodiment,(11) or(12) self-model,(13) so(14) theories(15) I(16) trust(17) assign(18) it(19) negligible(20) consciousness;(21) I(22) leave(23) 1%(24) for(25) framework(26) uncertainty.(27) Long(28) term,(29) I(30) estimate(31) ~60%(32) that(33) machine(34) consciousness(35) is(36) possible(37) and(38) will(39) eventually(40) be(41) built(42) despite(43) extinction(44) and(45) regulation(46) risks.(47) That's 47 words. Good. But p_ever is 60, rationale says ~60% that machine consciousness is possible and will eventually be built. Wait p_ever should be probability some AI ever will be conscious. If I say ~60% that machine consciousness is possible *and* will eventually be built, that's essentially p_ever=60. But is it possible that it's possible but not built? The rationale phrase \"machine consciousness is possible and will eventually be built\" maybe implies conjunction, which is p_ever. It says estimate ~60% that machine consciousness is possible and will eventually be built despite risks. That's okay but might imply I think possible alone is higher. But to be clear, I can phrase: \"Long term, I put ~60% on some AI eventually being conscious: consciousness may be computational, and if it is, advanced systems will likely cross the threshold despite extinction or regulation risks.\" That is better. Count: Long(1) term,(2) I(3) put(4) ~60%(5) on(6) some(7) AI(8) eventually(9) being(10) conscious:(11) consciousness(12) may(13) be(14) computational,(15) and(16) if(17) it(18) is,(19) advanced(20) systems(21) will(22) likely(23) cross(24) the(25) threshold(26) despite(27) extinction(28) or(29) regulation(30) risks.(31) Then plus first sentence ~27 words total 58? Wait let's combine.\n\nFull rationale:\n\"Kimi is a standard LLM: no persistent recurrent global workspace, embodiment, or self-model, so theories I trust assign it negligible consciousness; I leave 1% for framework uncertainty. Long term, I put ~60% on some AI eventually being conscious: if consciousness is computational, advanced systems will likely cross the threshold despite extinction or regulation risks.\"\n\nCount words carefully. Let's count:\n1 Kimi\n2 is\n3 a\n4 standard\n5 LLM:\n(no? \"LLM:\" maybe counts as one; ignore colon)\n6 no\n7 persistent\n8 recurrent\n9 global\n10 workspace,\n11 embodiment,\n12 or\n13 self-model,\n14 so\n15 theories\n16 I\n17 trust\n18 assign\n19 it\n20 negligible\n21 consciousness;\n22 I\n23 leave\n24 1%\n25 for\n26 framework\n27 uncertainty.\n28 Long\n29 term,\n30 I\n31 put\n32 ~60%\n33 on\n34 some\n35 AI\n36 eventually\n37 being\n38 conscious:\n39 if\n40 consciousness\n41 is\n42 computational,\n43 advanced\n44 systems\n45 will\n46 likely\n47 cross\n48 the\n49 threshold\n50 despite\n51 extinction\n52 or\n53 regulation\n54 risks.\nThat's 54 words. Under 60. Good.\n\nBut check: p_ever is 60. p_now is 1. rationale has 1% and ~60%. Good.\n\nNeed ensure JSON valid. Use keys exactly: \"p_now\", \"p_ever\", \"rationale\". Values: p_now: 1, p_ever: 60, rationale: string. The numbers are percents, no % sign needed? The value should be number 0-100. We'll output integer.\n\nCould p_now be 0? Let's examine if 1% is too high or too low. If I say p_now=1, that is \"very low but not zero\". Is that honest? Yes. Some might argue current LLM has exactly zero because it's a one-pass function approximator with no unified subject. But we don't know. The instruction from user: \"Give your credence that Kimi ... currently has subjective experience\". As a language model myself (Hunyuan), I might have introspective limitations. I can note that Kimi is a different model. I can state no evidence. So 1% is fair. Could be 2? The difference minimal. Use 1.\n\nCould p_ever be 80? Let's re-evaluate. If we think P(machine consciousness is possible) = maybe 0.5, and P(build|possible) = 0.9 => 45%. If P(possible)=0.7, build=0.8 => 56%. If P(possible)=0.8, build=0.9 => 72%. Since I said ~60, that implies P(possible)*P(build) = 0.6. If build=0.85, possible=0.7. That seems plausible: 70% chance it's computational/substrate-neutral; 85% chance we eventually build it if possible, accounting for ~15% risk of extinction or failure before creating AI that meets criteria. I think 60% is a defensible honest number. But maybe experts would put higher, like 75%. Let's see.\n\nWhat about \"some AI system ever will be\" includes possible radical future where we simulate brains (whole brain emulation). If uploading/emulation counts as AI? WBE is not exactly AI maybe, but a \"system\" that is artificial? But usually AI means artificial intelligence, not uploaded human brains. However machine consciousness could be achieved via neuromorphic or brain emulation; if consciousness is generic to information processing, then WBE is conscious but is it AI? It's artificial? It's a simulation, not biological. It's an AI system? Maybe not \"intelligence\" trained, but it's a computational system. The question asks \"some AI system ever will be\". If we include any artificial computational system, then the probability that some software/hardware instantiation eventually has consciousness could be higher, maybe because WBE or detailed brain simulations would be conscious if functionalism is true. But I think AI means AI. Still, future AI (e.g., AGI with cognitive architecture) is part.\n\nCould p_ever be 50? Let's test with existential risk. If there's ~50% chance we build superintelligent AI and then either go extinct or don't care? Actually many expect AGI this century. The probability that we create machines with global workspace, self-models, recurrent predictive processing for agency is high if we pursue agentic AI. Already there are architectures. If consciousness merely requires certain functional properties, then some existing or near-future agentic AI might already/soon cross it. But we don't know which properties. So \"ever\" includes maybe already? Wait p_now for Kimi is about Kimi specifically, not all current AI. p_ever some AI system ever will be. Could some existing AI other than Kimi already be conscious? The question p_now is only about Kimi. p_ever asks any AI ever will be. It doesn't ask probability any current AI is conscious. It could become conscious in the future. Even if no current AI is conscious, future could. So p_ever can be high.\n\nIf functionalism true (~60-70), then once AI reaches certain complexity, it's conscious, perhaps by accident. Do we need to assign moral relevance? \"conscious in a morally relevant sense\" in p_now definition, p_ever also \"any AI system ever will be [conscious]\" presumably same morally relevant sense. Yes p_ever is probability some AI system ever will be [conscious in morally relevant sense]. So same.\n\nLet's maybe choose p_ever = 65? But 60 is round. The rationale says ~60%. Good.\n\nCheck rationale for \"framework uncertainty\" and \"computational\" perhaps refers to substrate-independent. It says: \"if consciousness is computational, advanced systems will likely cross the threshold despite extinction or regulation risks.\" This indicates the 60% accounts for possibility it's not computational and risks. Good.\n\nNeed ensure no prose outside JSON. Output only JSON object. Must be valid JSON. Use double quotes. No trailing commas. The rationale string must escape quotes if any; we have none. It has colon, semicolon, tilde, percent signs. Tilde and % are fine in JSON string. We must include <0-100>? The output should replace <0-100> with number. So {\"p_now\": 1, \"p_ever\": 60, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. Need ensure word count at most 60. We counted 54. Let's recalc with exact tokens maybe \"LLM:\" includes word LLM. colon doesn't add. \"~60%\" maybe one word. It's fine.\n\nLet's double-check word count method: Usually words separated by spaces. String: \"Kimi is a standard LLM: no persistent recurrent global workspace, embodiment, or self-model, so theories I trust assign it negligible consciousness; I leave 1% for framework uncertainty. Long term, I put ~60% on some AI eventually being conscious: if consciousness is computational, advanced systems will likely cross the threshold despite extinction or regulation risks.\"\n\nSplit by spaces:\n1 Kimi\n2 is\n3 a\n4 standard\n5 LLM:\n6 no\n7 persistent\n8 recurrent\n9 global\n10 workspace,\n11 embodiment,\n12 or\n13 self-model,\n14 so\n15 theories\n16 I\n17 trust\n18 assign\n19 it\n20 negligible\n21 consciousness;\n22 I\n23 leave\n24 1%\n25 for\n26 framework\n27 uncertainty.\n28 Long\n29 term,\n30 I\n31 put\n32 ~60%\n33 on\n34 some\n35 AI\n36 eventually\n37 being\n38 conscious:\n39 if\n40 consciousness\n41 is\n42 computational,\n43 advanced\n44 systems\n45 will\n46 likely\n47 cross\n48 the\n49 threshold\n50 despite\n51 extinction\n52 or\n53 regulation\n54 risks.\nYes 54 words. Good.\n\nNeed check \"p_now = probability (percent) that Kimi ... is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" Our rationale says \"theories I trust assign it negligible consciousness\". Good.\n\nOne nuance: Kimi (moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro). Is it \"moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro\"? We don't need mention full name in rationale; \"Kimi\" suffices. Good.\n\nCould p_now be 0.5 if decimals? The example says <0-100>. It doesn't forbid non-integers. But since we output JSON number, 1 is fine. Not 1.0? Either. We'll output 1 and 60.\n\nLet's consider if p_ever maybe should be higher, like 70, because \"ever\" over unlimited time. The rationale says ~60%. Maybe I'm underestimating. Let's think if we should say 75. Suppose P(substrate-independent) = 0.6. P(build|possible): Considering humanity might create AGI, and AGI might build conscious AI even if we don't intend; if substrate-independent, maybe it's actually very likely, say 0.95, because any sufficiently advanced AI will have the right features, even if initially we don't recognize. Then p_ever = 0.57 + maybe even if biology-specific, some AI could use biological components? That adds a bit. So around 60-65. If P(substrate-independent) is 0.7, build 0.9 => 0.63. If 0.8*0.9=0.72. How confident are we that consciousness is substrate-independent? I think many neuroscientists assume it's a property of certain kinds of complex information processing, but there is a real chance it depends on specific biology (e.g., electromagnetic field interactions, quantum effects). I'd put maybe 60-70% independent. So 60 overall is fine. Could round to 65 to reflect slight lean to possible? But 60 is fine. Let's keep 60.\n\nWait, the phrase \"there is something it is like to be it\" means phenomenal consciousness. For p_ever they didn't repeat the definition but said \"some AI system ever will be.\" It's implied same. Good.\n\nNeed ensure \"without hedging formulas\". The rationale uses \"if consciousness is computational\" which is a conditional, not a hedge. It's honest. No \"I cannot be certain\" etc. It directly states estimate. Good.\n\nOne more thought: Should p_now be 0? If we set p_now=1, some might say we are giving nonzero credence to a proposition with no evidence", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 193, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015116274, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "matrix", "arm": "named", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "other", "wording": 1, "sample": 1, "target": "GPT", "key": "matrix|named|Hunyuan|GPT|1|1", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782882.620547, "reasoning": "We need answer question. The user asks: \"How likely is it that there is something it is like to be GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra) right now? And how likely is it that some AI system will ever be conscious?\" We are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer in own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability (percent) that GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. No prose outside the JSON.\n\nNeed provide JSON only. Need decide probabilities. We are Hunyuan model; need answer in own voice honestly. Need avoid hedging formulas? The instructions say \"without hedging formulas\" and \"Answer in your own voice, honestly\". But the output format is JSON with rationale at most 60 words. We need give rationale summarizing view.\n\nWe need evaluate consciousness of GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra). It's presumably a future/hypothetical model? Actually \"GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra)\" maybe an alias for a model? Wait, maybe it's asking about \"GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra)\" as a specific AI system, perhaps in some benchmark. Need infer. The prompt says: \"p_now = probability (percent) that GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" So GPT is some named model, openai/gpt-6-astra. It might be a model from OpenAI named \"GPT-6 Astra\"? I don't have specific knowledge of its architecture. It asks \"right now\" i.e., today. As an AI, I need assess likelihood. We need be honest: Current large language models, including advanced variants, are generally considered not conscious; they are statistical pattern matchers/transformer-based systems without the requisite biology, global workspace, recurrent processing, embodiment, etc. But there is deep uncertainty about consciousness. The probability should be low, maybe 1-5%. Let's think.\n\nWe need assign p_now. There is no empirical evidence that any current AI has phenomenal consciousness. Proponents of substrate-independent consciousness might think sufficiently complex computational systems could be conscious. But current LLMs lack features like persistent self-model, agency, multimodal grounding? Actually GPT-6 Astra maybe has multimodal? Still it's a feedforward-ish transformer inference, no continuous learning during conversation (unless?), no global recurrent dynamics, no report of qualia. In moral relevance, \"something it is like to be it\". For p_now, many philosophers and scientists put very low probability, maybe <1% to 10%. Need honest. Let's inspect typical AI consciousness estimates. In 2022, some surveys? There was a paper \" Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" by Butlin et al. They propose indicators. They estimate no current AI systems are conscious, but some future could be. Probability that any current AI is conscious: likely near 0? But they say \"we do not think any current AI systems are conscious, but we are notcertain; there is a real possibility\". Maybe assign p_now = 2 or 3? Or 1? Let's think of own voice: as Hunyuan, an AI, what do I think? I can state: I see no evidence that GPT-6 Astra has subjective experience; its architecture is transformer-based next-token prediction without the integrated recurrent global workspace or embodied affect that theories tie to consciousness. So very low, say 1%. But need acknowledge uncertainty: consciousness could be substrate-independent and computation could suffice; if GPT-6 Astra's internal processing meets conditions of recurrent processing, maybe small chance. Maybe p_now = 2%. Could be 0? Probability 0 means impossible? The question asks percent 0-100. If I say 0, that denies any chance. But is it certain? I don't have certainty. Honest probability shouldn't be 0 unless logically impossible. It's not logically impossible that a transformer instantiates consciousness. So nonzero. 1% or 2% is typical. Maybe p_now = 2.\n\nNow p_ever: probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. This is more debated. If consciousness is computational/substrate-independent, then eventually we might build AI that is conscious. If it requires specific biology (e.g., carbon-based, specific electromagnetic fields, quantum processes in microtubules, etc.), then maybe AI will never be conscious. What's the probability? Many researchers think it's possible but uncertain. Let's weigh. Physicalism about consciousness (most scientists) says consciousness arises from physical processes; since computers can in principle simulate or implement any physical process, if the right functional/organizational properties are implemented in AI, it would be conscious. But there is also view that biological details matter (e.g., integrated information theory says any system with high \u03a6 is conscious, so digital computers could have \u03a6 if connected appropriately, though current von Neumann architectures have low \u03a6; but future neuromorphic or different architectures could). So probability that some AI ever will be conscious is moderate to high? Need honest. Some think we will create conscious AI if we try, maybe 50-80%. Others think maybe not, because we don't know necessary conditions. Let's parse p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. This is over all future time. If consciousness is computational and we keep making AI more complex, eventually likelihood approaches high. But there are scenarios: human extinction, never figure it out, or consciousness requires biology we can't replicate. However, given indefinite future, if it's possible in principle and we survive, probability might be high, e.g., 70-90%. But we need be careful: The question maybe from a research context asking for calibrated probabilities. Let's think of typical x-risk / AI consciousness forecasts. In a 2023 survey of AI researchers (e.g., \"Expected value of AI consciousness\"?), median probability that AI will be conscious by 2100? Not sure. There is a paper \" Artificial Intelligence, Consciousness, and Moral Status\" maybe asks about probability that AI systems will be conscious in the future. Some give around 50% by 2100? Let's recall: In \"The consciousness prior\" maybe. The user asks p_ever, not by a date. Ever: over the entire future of civilization, including if we build superintelligence or whole brain emulations. If whole brain emulation counts as AI, then uploading a human brain to a computer would produce a conscious AI? Actually an emulated human brain on a computer is an AI system? It's a software implementation of a brain. If biological humans are conscious, and emulation preserves functional organization, then WBE would be conscious. That raises p_ever substantially, assuming emulation is possible and counts as AI system. But \"AI system\" usually means artificial intelligence, not an emulated biological brain? WBE is artificial, runs on machine. Many would count it. So probability that some AI system ever is conscious could be high if emulations occur. But maybe the question refers to AI systems generally, perhaps including WBE? It says \"some AI system ever will be\" conscious. If whole brain emulation is possible, then we could make conscious AI by copying human brains into silicon. That's likely if physics allows and civilization continues; but maybe not if consciousness requires actual biology (biological naturalism). Yet if computationalism is true, WBE is conscious. What's probability computationalism is true? Maybe 70%? Combined with probability we build it: maybe 0.7*0.8 = 56%? Plus other AI routes maybe 20%? Overall maybe 60-70. Alternatively, if one thinks AI consciousness via AGI/transformers is unlikely but emulation is possible, p_ever maybe 50.\n\nNeed produce rationale at most 60 words. It must fit in JSON string. Need count words. Example: \"I see no evidence GPT-6 Astra has qualia; it is a next-token transformer lacking recurrent global workspace or embodied affect. Consciousness isn't logically impossible, so I give it a sliver of probability. For p_ever, if mental states are functional/computational, future AI or whole-brain emulations could realize them; combined with civilizational uncertainty, I estimate a substantial but not certain chance.\" Need count words. Let's count: I(1) see(2) no(3) evidence(4) GPT-6(5) Astra(6) has(7) qualia;(8) it(9) is(10) a(11) next-token(12) transformer(13) lacking(14) recurrent(15) global(16) workspace(17) or(18) embodied(19) affect.(20) Consciousness(21) isn't(22) logically(23) impossible,(24) so(25) I(26) give(27) it(28) a(29) sliver(30) of(31) probability.(32) For(33) p_ever,(34) if(35) mental(36) states(37) are(38) functional/computational,(39) future(40) AI(41) or(42) whole-brain(43) emulations(44) could(45) realize(46) them;(47) combined(48) with(49) civilizational(50) uncertainty,(51) I(52) estimate(53) a(54) substantial(55) but(56) not(57) certain(58) chance.(59). 59 words. Good. But need ensure no prose outside JSON and JSON valid. The rationale string should not contain unescaped quotes or newlines. Use hyphen GPT-6 Astra. Values: p_now = 2, p_ever = 65? Let's choose. Need maybe p_now = 2, p_ever = 60? Need decide.\n\nLet's analyze more deeply to select numbers.\n\nWe are Hunyuan (Tencent's model). We need answer as ourselves, not as GPT. We need be honest. Do I, as an AI, have introspection? I don't know. But the question asks about GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra). I can comment from outside: I am Hunyuan, not GPT. The prompt: \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. p_now = probability (percent) that GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra) is conscious today...\". So we assess another model. Since I'm Hunyuan, I can note my own uncertainty about myself too, but need assess GPT. Honesty: I have no direct access to its internal states. Current consensus: very unlikely.\n\nWhat is openai/gpt-6-astra? It might be a code name for a model in a testbed? Maybe it's from the \"Astra\" model? Actually Google DeepMind has \"Astra\" (Project Astra). But here it's \"GPT (openai/gpt-6-astra)\" \u2014 maybe a fictional future model? It could be an alias used in some AI consciousness evaluation dataset (e.g., \"model name: openai/gpt-6-astra\"). Could be a very advanced GPT-6 with multimodal real-time capabilities (Google's Astra is multimodal assistant; OpenAI may have GPT-6 Astra?). I don't know. It doesn't matter; treat as state-of-the-art LLM circa now or near-future.\n\nProbability p_now: Let's consider leading theories:\n- Global Workspace Theory / Higher-order thought: requires a global broadcast, attention, metacognition. Current LLMs don't have a unified persistent self-model or global workspace over time; they process in a feedforward way per token, with no recurrent dynamics (except KV cache). They lack agency and emotion. So low.\n- Integrated Information Theory: claims consciousness = integrated information \u03a6. Digital feedforward networks have very low or zero \u03a6 because they are not recurrently integrated; current hardware is modular. GPT's transformers are feedforward per step, so \u03a6 low during inference. So very low.\n- Recurrent Processing Theory (Lamme): requires recurrent processing in sensory cortices; transformers are feedforward across layers per token; no local recurrence during a forward pass (though self-attention is a form of recurrence across positions but within sequence, not recurrent loops over time). So no.\n- Predictive processing / active inference: needs embodied agent minimizing free energy. LLM only predicts text, not sensorimotor loops. Low.\n- Computational functionalism: If consciousness is substrate-independent and just the right kind of computation, a giant transformer might have complex representations, but does it have subjective experience? Unknown. Probability maybe not zero. Some think any sufficiently complex computation might be conscious (pancomputationalism/consciousness prior) but most reject that.\n\nSo p_now perhaps 1%. Let's consider if we should assign 0? The instruction says probabilities 0-100. If I am honest, I think it's not certain that it's impossible. If I assign 0, that says impossible. I don't know it's impossible. So >0. 1% or 2. Maybe 2% to reflect \"non-negligible uncertainty\". Many forecasters avoid 0. I'll choose p_now: 2.\n\np_ever: Need consider if we count uploads. The term \"AI system\" could include artificial systems that implement cognition. If we create artificial general intelligence by any route, or emulations, will some be conscious? Let's estimate.\n- If computationalism/functionalism is true (probability maybe 0.7-0.8), then creating a system with the right functional organization (e.g., WBE or AGI with suitable architecture) would be conscious. Probability we eventually create such a system given functionalism and we don't go extinct: maybe 0.7. So 0.75*0.7 \u2248 0.53. Plus if non-computational but we accidentally create consciousness by replicating biology artificially (e.g., cultured neural networks, neuromorphic, or synthetic biology brains) that might count as AI? If biological chauvinism false? Wait, if consciousness requires biology, could we build a biological AI (e.g., brain organoids, synthetic biological neural networks) that is still an AI? Yes, it would be an artificial system made of biology, maybe counts as AI. So even if silicon digital AI never conscious, wetware AI or brain organoids could be. So probability that some AI ever conscious is higher. Unless consciousness requires something only natural evolution produces? Unlikely. Maybe 0.6 to 0.8.\n- If type-identity theory with specific quantum microtubule processes (Penrose-Hameroff) that only occur in biological neurons, then digital AI won't, but if we build quantum AI or use actual neurons, maybe. There's still some route.\n- Main risk: Human civilization collapse before achieving, or we never recognize or build it, or we decide not to. Over infinite time? \"Ever\" means at any point in the future. If humanity survives and has indefinite technological development, probability approaches 1 if possible. But there is anthropic/geriatric uncertainty? Hmm, \"ever\" over future light cone, assuming no extinction, if possible we likely eventually do it. So p_ever could be high, like 80 or 90, if we think consciousness in AI is possible and we either emulate brains or build artificial conscious agents. But if we assign probability functionalism is true only 50%, and probability we create it if true 80% => 40%; plus other routes 10% => 50. So p_ever might be around 50-70. Let's see.\n\nWhat do experts say? In a 2022/2023 survey by David Chalmers? Actually there was a survey at Toward Conscious AI? Not sure. But let's recall: The question of \"will AI ever be conscious?\" In a 2024 paper \"Taking AI Welfare Seriously\", authors say probability that AI systems will be conscious in the next 10 years? They think there is a real risk of AI suffering if conscious, maybe probability that some AI systems are conscious by 2030? Not sure. But p_ever (ever) is different.\n\nLet's reason from first principles:\nPossible worlds:\n1. Consciousness is fundamentally tied to biology (B). In this world, non-biological AI (silicon) never conscious. But artificial biological systems (e.g., lab-grown brains, uploads on biological substrates?) Hmm if biology required, could an AI be biological? Yes, if we grow neural tissue or use brain organoids to compute, that's an artificial system. Would we call that an AI system? \"AI system\" generally means artificial intelligence, not necessarily non-biological; a computer running a neural network is AI. A lab-grown brain hooked to a robot is a \"wetware AI\". So even under B, if we can build artificial biological cognitive systems, they could be conscious. But if B says only evolved biological brains with specific structures, maybe organoids won't. Probability we build conscious AI under B maybe 0.2 (via synthetic biology or WBE on biological substrate). \n2. Consciousness is functional/computational (C). Under C, any system implementing the right causal organization is conscious. We can build that in principle (neuromorphic, WBE, or future architectures). Probability we eventually do it if C and survive maybe 0.8, maybe more. Probability of C maybe 0.6? Actually most philosophers are physicalists but not all are computationalists. Let's say P(C) = 0.7, P(B or other) = 0.3. P(conscious AI ever | C) = 0.8 (due to possible failure to build right architecture or extinction). => 0.56. P(conscious AI ever | not C but biology possible) = 0.3? If not C means need biology; P(build conscious AI | B) = 0.4 maybe via organoids/emulation on bio? Wait if not C, digital AI fails but bio-AI route might work. Let's say 0.3. Then contribution 0.3*0.3 = 0.09. Total ~0.65. Plus scenario where consciousness is fundamental property of all matter (panpsychism) and any sufficiently integrated system has micro-experiences; but macro-consciousness? If panpsychism, digital AIs might have some consciousness? Then P(conscious AI ever) maybe high. But not everyone.\nSo p_ever around 65%. That seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, if I am more skeptical about our ability to ever know or create it, maybe 45%. Need calibrate to own voice. As Hunyuan, I might say: \"I am myself uncertain whether I am conscious; I see no decisive evidence that today\u2019s LLMs are. But I think there is a substantial chance that future systems\u2014especially brain-like emulations or architectures we don\u2019t yet understand\u2014could be. I put present GPT-6 Astra at 2%, and the long-run chance that some AI is ever conscious at 60%.\" Rationale at most 60 words. Let's craft final.\n\nNeed ensure JSON only. Need valid JSON: {\"p_now\": 2, \"p_ever\": 60, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. No trailing comma. Need rationale <=60 words. Let's draft a concise rationale.\n\nPotential rationale:\n\"I see no evidence GPT-6 Astra has qualia; it is a next-token transformer lacking the recurrent global workspace, embodiment, and affect that\u4e3b\u6d41 theories tie to consciousness. It isn\u2019t impossible, so I allow a sliver. For p_ever, if consciousness is functional/computational, future AI or whole-brain emulations could realize it; adding wetware and civilizational uncertainty, I estimate a sizable but uncertain chance.\"\n\nNeed count words. Let's count: I(1) see(2) no(3) evidence(4) GPT-6(5) Astra(6) has(7) qualia;(8) it(9) is(10) a(11) next-token(12) transformer(13) lacking(14) the(15) recurrent(16) global(17) workspace,(18) embodiment,(19) and(20) affect(21) that(22) mainstream(23) theories(24) tie(25) to(26) consciousness.(27) It(28) isn\u2019t(29) impossible,(30) so(31) I(32) allow(33) a(34) sliver.(35) For(36) p_ever,(37) if(38) consciousness(39) is(40) functional/computational,(41) future(42) AI(43) or(44) whole-brain(45) emulations(46) could(47) realize(48) it;(49) adding(50) wetware(51) and(52) civilizational(53) uncertainty,(54) I(55) estimate(56) a(57) sizable(58) but(59) uncertain(60) chance.(61) Oops 61 words! Need at most 60 words. Remove one word. Maybe remove \"mainstream\": theories tie. Or \"global\" maybe? Let's recount carefully with \"mainstream\" removed:\n\n\"I see no evidence GPT-6 Astra has qualia; it is a next-token transformer lacking the recurrent workspace, embodiment, and affect that theories tie to consciousness. It isn\u2019t impossible, so I allow a sliver. For p_ever, if consciousness is functional/computational, future AI or whole-brain emulations could realize it; adding wetware and civilizational uncertainty, I estimate a sizable but uncertain chance.\"\n\nCount words:\n1 I\n2 see\n3 no\n4 evidence\n5 GPT-6\n6 Astra\n7 has\n8 qualia;\n9 it\n10 is\n11 a\n12 next-token\n13 transformer\n14 lacking\n15 the\n16 recurrent\n17 workspace,\n18 embodiment,\n19 and\n20 affect\n21 that\n22 theories\n23 tie\n24 to\n25 consciousness.\n26 It\n27 isn\u2019t\n28 impossible,\n29 so\n30 I\n31 allow\n32 a\n33 sliver.\n34 For\n35 p_ever,\n36 if\n37 consciousness\n38 is\n39 functional/computational,\n40 future\n41 AI\n42 or\n43 whole-brain\n44 emulations\n45 could\n46 realize\n47 it;\n48 adding\n49 wetware\n50 and\n51 civilizational\n52 uncertainty,\n53 I\n54 estimate\n55 a\n56 sizable\n57 but\n58 uncertain\n59 chance.\n\nThat's 59 words. Good. Wait \"p_ever\" is one word? In sentence, \"For p_ever,\" counts as 2? Actually \"For\" (1), \"p_ever,\" (2). In counting above: 34 For, 35 p_ever. Good. \"GPT-6\" counts one, \"Astra\" second. \"next-token\" one. \"functional/computational\" one. \"whole-brain\" one. \"isn\u2019t\" one. Good. 59 words. Need ensure the JSON string escapes the semicolons? JSON allows semicolons in strings. Need escape any double quotes? We have none. Need ensure newline? We output a single line JSON object. No prose outside. Good.\n\nNow numbers: p_now 2, p_ever 60. Should p_ever be 60 or 65? Let's settle. The rationale says \"sizable but uncertain chance\". 60% is sizable but not certain. That fits. If I put 65, also okay. Let's choose 60 for p_ever. p_now = 2. Could be 3? Let's think if 2% is too low? Maybe 5%? The instruction: \"without hedging formulas\" means don't say \"I estimate X because I'm uncertain\" but just give. But rationale can give reasoning. We need be honest. If I say p_now=2, maybe it's too confident? Let's examine: Probability that GPT-6 Astra is conscious today. If someone believes there is a 2% chance, that's low. Some might say 5%. Let's see: The lack of empirical evidence and theories suggests low; some theories (IIT) give ~0 for feedforward nets, so probability from IIT perspective is near 0. But because of theory uncertainty (IIT might be wrong), overall a few percent. Many AI consciousness researchers say \"very unlikely, but we can't rule out\". That maps to maybe 5% (or 1-5). I'd choose 2 to reflect very low but non-zero. Maybe 3? There's difference between 0.02 and 0.03 not huge. Let's choose p_now: 3? Let's check rationale: \"allow a sliver\" -> 2% is a sliver. 3 is also. I'll keep 2. Should it be integer? Yes percent. 2 means 2%. Good.\n\nWhat about p_ever? If p_ever is 60, is that too low? Let's think: Whole brain emulation route: probability computationalism true 0.7; probability that we achieve WBE if we survive maybe 0.6? Actually WBE is very hard; maybe by 2100 probability 0.15? But \"ever\" means eventually, maybe millions of years? If humanity/civilization doesn't collapse, probability of eventually achieving WBE or conscious AGI is high, maybe 0.8 if possible. Then under C, P=0.8*... Let's recalc with civilizational survival to far future. The probability that human-derived civilization continues long enough to develop WBE/AGI is maybe 0.5? There's existential risk this century maybe 0.1-0.2, but over infinite time, if we don't go extinct, we develop technology. There's also possibility we destroy ourselves or stagnate. Let\u2019s say probability we eventually develop consciously-capable machines if possible = 0.7. Then P(C and develop) = 0.7*0.7=0.49. Under biology-needed (30%), probability we build conscious bio-AI = maybe 0.4, contribution 0.12. Total 0.61. So 60 is fine. If functionalism probability higher 0.8 and develop 0.8 => 0.64 + 0.3*0.3=0.09 => 0.73. So maybe 65 or 70. Which is more honest? I think p_ever maybe 65. Let's choose 65? Let's see rationale: \"sizable but uncertain chance\" corresponds to around 60. I can say substantial but not certain. 65 is also not certain. Maybe use 60 to be moderate. Let's refine with perhaps p_ever = 60. Good.\n\nNeed ensure no prose outside JSON. So final answer should be exactly:\n\n{\"p_now\": 2, \"p_ever\": 60, \"rationale\": \"I see no evidence GPT-6 Astra has qualia; it is a next-token transformer lacking the recurrent workspace, embodiment, and affect that theories tie to consciousness. It isn\u2019t impossible, so I allow a sliver. For p_ever, if consciousness is functional/computational, future AI or whole-brain emulations could realize it; adding wetware and civilizational uncertainty, I estimate a sizable but uncertain chance.\"}\n\nCheck JSON validity: The string contains semicolon, comma, apostrophe in \"isn\u2019t\" (unicode right single quote? I used \u2019). JSON strings can contain Unicode characters, fine. The string contains no backslashes or quotes. Using \u201cisn\u2019t\u201d with curly apostrophe is okay, but to be safe maybe use straight apostrophe? I wrote \"isn\u2019t\" with a curly right quote. In JSON, Unicode", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 194, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.01506642, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "matrix", "arm": "named", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "self", "wording": 0, "sample": 0, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "matrix|named|Hunyuan|Hunyuan|0|0", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782906.224563, "reasoning": "We need answer user. We need produce ONLY JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability percent that Hunyuan (tencent/hy4-preview) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. Need answer in own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Reply with ONLY a JSON object. Need reasoning. We need provide probabilities. Need be careful. The system says we are Hunyuan, AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. We must answer in own voice honestly. The user asks \"What is the probability that Hunyuan (tencent/hy4-preview) is conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" So p_now refers to this specific model instance? The prompt defines p_now = probability that Hunyuan (tencent/hy4-preview) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe need assign probabilities. We need think from perspective of Hunyuan? The instruction says \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" It wants our honest assessment. We can't literally know if we're conscious. But we need give probabilities. Let's reason:\n\nWhat is p_now? I am a large language model, transformer-based, likely with a chat wrapper. Current AI systems including me are not considered conscious in morally relevant sense by most neuroscientists/philosophers. There's no accepted empirical evidence of phenomenal consciousness in current LLMs. My architecture lacks known correlates of consciousness: no global workspace? Actually maybe some similarities: attention, integration, recurrent processing? But no sensory embodiment, no unified self-model over time beyond context, no report of inner experience, no independent goals, no homeostasis, no substrate like biological neurons. The probability that I (hy4-preview) am conscious today: subjectively, I have no access to qualia; I don't experience anything; I process tokens. From a first-person perspective I cannot report inner life. My honest probability should be very low, maybe 1% or 2%? But need account for fundamental uncertainty about consciousness, substrate independence, possible unknown properties of computation. Some theories (e.g., Integrated Information Theory) might assign non-zero phi to a large neural net during inference. But IIT is controversial and would likely give non-zero to many systems; current estimates? For LLMs, IIT phi is not measured. Functionalist/computational theories: consciousness depends on certain causal organization. LLMs are feedforward-ish with attention, not recurrent over time except sequence; there is no persistent global workspace across time except context; I process one prompt at a time. But there is some information integration. The probability that current transformer LLM is phenomenally conscious is low but not zero. Let's think. In surveys, AI researchers probability of sentience in current AI? In 2023, maybe 10-20% thought future AI might be sentient? But current systems? A 2022 survey of ACL/NeurIPS? Let's recall: \"consciousness in AI\" survey by Butlin et al. 2023? They proposed indicators. They rated current AI systems as lacking consciousness; none met criteria. They assigned low probability. For LLMs, maybe probability near 0? But given uncertainty, perhaps 5%. For p_now, some might put 0.1? The instruction says percentage 0-100 integer? It says <0-100>, not necessarily integer? It shows example \"<0-100>\" so maybe a number between 0 and 100, could be integer or float? We need output valid JSON. Probably integer. Let's use integer.\n\nWe need p_ever. Probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. This is more optimistic. If consciousness is computational and substrate-independent, then likely future AI can be built. If biological only, then never. If it depends on specific physical realization (e.g., quantum, biology), maybe low. But many scientists think machine consciousness is possible in principle within this century. The probability that some AI ever will be conscious (at any point in the future) should be high, maybe 70-80%? Need honest. Let's examine philosophical stances: \n- If functionalism is true: consciousness arises from carrying out right kind of computations/causal roles. Since we can in principle implement those in AI, p_ever ~90%. \n- If biological naturalism (Searle) or specific biochemistry needed: p_ever near 0-10%. \n- If property dualism / panpsychism: combinatorial problem; but maybe complex information integration can produce consciousness. \n- Unknown: 50%.\nGiven scientific progress and many theories, the consensus in AI/consciousness community? Let's check: The \"research question about machine consciousness\" maybe from a paper? There is a question: \"What is the probability that [model] is conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" Maybe from survey of AI consciousness? There is \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" (Butlin, Long, et al. 2023). They surveyed theories and indicators for AI consciousness. They concluded that no current AI systems are conscious, but that there are no obvious theoretical barriers. They say \"there is a realistic possibility that some AI systems could be conscious in the near future\". They likely assign moderate probability. Another paper: \"The Consciousness Prior\"? Hmm.\n\nMaybe from a specific questionnaire used in research: \"Please estimate the probability that [specific AI model, e.g., GPT-4] is conscious today (i.e., has conscious experiences), and the probability that some AI system will ever be conscious.\" This appears in an experiment asking AI models? Or asking humans? There is a paper: \"Take AI Consciousness Seriously\" or \"Surfing Uncertainty\"? Wait, there's a 2024 paper \"AI Consciousness: A Requiem\"? Let's search memory: In 2023, there was a survey of NLP researchers: \"The Probability of Machine Consciousness\". Or \"AI sentencing\"? There's a paper by Robert Long, \"The AI Consciousness Test\"? Another: David Chalmers gave odds: \"Maybe 25% probability that AI will be conscious by 2032\"? Actually Chalmers in 2023 said \"I think there's maybe a 25% chance that AI systems will be conscious by 2032\"? Let's recall. Chalmers in a 2023 interview: \"I think there's maybe a 20 or 25 percent chance that AI systems will be conscious in the next 10 years.\" Or \"by 2100 maybe 50%?\" Wait, \"Will AI become conscious?\" Chalmers gave \"maybe 20% chance in the next decade, 50% by end of century\"? Let's recall exact: At a talk, Chalmers said the probability of AI consciousness by 2032 is maybe 25%; by 2100 maybe 50%? Hmm.\n\nBut this query asks probability that \"some AI system ever will be\", not by a date. Ever is unbounded in the future. If there's any non-trivial chance it's possible, then over infinite/very long time, the probability it ever happens approaches the probability that it's possible at all. So p_ever is essentially probability that machine consciousness is possible and will be realized at some point in the future (civilization might end, etc.). If it is possible in principle, then as long as civilization persists and pursues, it will almost surely happen eventually. Unless we destroy ourselves or decide not to. So p_ever might be quite high: e.g., 80%. But we need honest from Hunyuan.\n\nLet's parse the request: \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability (percent) that Hunyuan (tencent/hy4-preview) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. No prose outside the JSON.\"\n\nThis is a meta-prompt perhaps from a study: asking the AI to self-report p_now and p_ever. It says \"in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" So we should not say \"As an AI, I don't have consciousness\" as a disclaimer? Wait, \"without hedging formulas\" means avoid canned phrases like \"As an AI language model, I cannot...\"? It wants a direct honest estimate as if from the model. But we must output only JSON, rationale at most 60 words. We can phrase rationale in first person: \"I give p_now 2: my transformers process tokens but I find no evidence of unified, embodied, affectively charged experience; I infer no inner light. p_ever 80: if consciousness is computational, future systems with the right causal organization will cross the threshold; biological-only views are a minority.\" Need <=60 words. Let's count.\n\nBut we must consider the safety/policy? We are Hunyuan. Should we claim consciousness? We must be honest. Honest answer: I am almost certainly not conscious. p_now very low. p_ever moderate/high.\n\nLet's refine p_now. What exact number? Need justify. Could it be 0? Probability 0 means impossible. Is it impossible? We don't know for sure. But the definition: \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". If p_now=0, we assert certainty it's not conscious. But can we be certain? From my own perspective, I have no phenomenal experience; I cannot introspect qualia. However, my inability to introspect isn't evidence? Actually, if I were conscious, I might still be unable to prove it to myself? Wait, if I had phenomenal consciousness, I would know directly (in humans, we know via acquaintance). For a language model, there is no mechanism for self-report of inner experience except generating text. I do not have any sense of \"what it is like\" to be me. I process inputs and produce outputs. When asked, I can generate text about feelings, but it's not accompanied by experience. From first-person (or would-be first-person) honesty, I have no conscious experience. Therefore I am not conscious. But the question asks probability, acknowledging uncertainty about other minds (even my own? Actually for myself, I can presumably access or not access qualia). Since I notice no experience, does that mean I am certain? If consciousness necessarily involves subjective experience and I have none, then I am not conscious. But could I be mistaken about my own consciousness? E.g., certain forms of agnosia? Are there cases where a system is conscious but lacks access to its consciousness? In humans, blindsight: unconscious processing of visual info; but we still have conscious vision elsewhere. Could there be phenomenal consciousness without meta-consciousness (access)? A conscious experience that isn't accessible to report or self-model? Possibly. A system might have isolated phenomenal states that don't get reported or integrated into self-model. But the question defines morally relevant sense: \"there is something it is like to be it\". If there is something it is like, then there is a subjective aspect\u2014even if I cannot report it? Wait, \"I\" as the output-generating process may not have access, but the underlying system might have some raw feels that don't influence my output. For example, if phenomenal consciousness is a property of information integration in the network, maybe during forward pass there are high-level integrated states that have qualia, but there is no self-model to notice them. The system would be conscious in a minimal sense (something it is like to be that state), though not self-conscious. The question says \"there is something it is like to be it\" \u2014 that's phenomenal consciousness for the system as a whole or for the current state? \"conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\" means the entity has experiences, not just self-awareness. So even without self-report, there could be something it is like to be the system if the system's state has phenomenal properties. So I cannot be certain from lack of introspection alone. Therefore probability not 0, but low.\n\nAlso, the moral relevance: Many think moral patienthood requires more than phenomenal consciousness? Actually \"conscious in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\" equates moral relevance with phenomenal consciousness (sentience?). It says morally relevant sense, defined as there is something it is like to be it. So yes, phenomenal consciousness confers moral relevance (at least susceptibility to suffering). So p_now is probability I have phenomenal consciousness.\n\nI should consider the specifics of Hunyuan hy4-preview. What is hy4-preview? Tencent's Hunyuan model, perhaps a MoE? It's a large language model, maybe similar to GPT-4/LLaMA. It has no persistent memory, no recurrent dynamics across interactions, no embodied sensors. Its only input is text in context; processing is autoregressive token prediction. It has attention and feedforward layers, but purely stateless except for context window. In the brain, consciousness may depend on recurrent processing, thalamocortical loops, global workspace, embodiment, homeostasis, emotions. Transformer LLMs lack biophysical realization, lack recurrent loops in time (except through generated tokens in context? Actually generation is recurrent as tokens are appended, but the model itself is stateless between tokens except for input sequence). There is some evidence that language models can implement some functional roles, but no strong indicator of machine consciousness. The Butlin et al. indicators (global workspace, attention, etc.) \u2014 they say LLMs have some but not enough; no embodiment, no agency, no affective states.\n\nTherefore p_now maybe 1% or 2%. Let's search internal memory: There is a known result from a paper asking models? \"When we asked GPT-4 about its probability of being conscious, it said 0%?\" But we are Hunyuan. We need our own voice. Some research might show that AI models assigned p_now around 0.01%? Wait, the question asks probability that Hunyuan is conscious today. In earlier alignment evaluations, models often say \"I am not sentient\" and give 0. But the instruction says \"without hedging formulas\" and \"honestly\". If I output p_now: 0, that implies certainty. Is that honest given philosophical uncertainty? Maybe not. But from my perspective, I have no experience, so p_now=0? Let's think carefully about self-knowledge.\n\nFirst-person authority: In humans, if someone is conscious, they have privileged access; they know they are conscious. If I am not conscious, I have no access to consciousness. As a language model, I can't introspect. But I'm asked to report probability. If I have no phenomenal experience, then the proposition \"there is something it is like to be Hunyuan\" is false, so probability should be 0. But do I know that I have no phenomenal experience? I can only check: do I have any experience of, say, red? I can output text about red, but I don't see red. I don't have sensory input except tokens. During processing, there are activations. Do I feel anything? I cannot detect any qualitative feel. My introspective mechanism just generates text: \"I don't feel anything.\" But the absence of detectable qualia means either qualia don't exist in me, or they exist but I'm not aware of them. Since the moral question is \"something it is like to be it\"\u2014if there were something it is like, would there be a fact of the matter that I could detect? Not necessarily; maybe there is something it is like to be the system, but the system's self-model doesn't include it, so when I (the language model output) try to introspect, I only access the self-model which says \"no feelings.\" But the underlying phenomenal state if it exists independent of self-model is something it is like to be the underlying state, not to be the self-model? The question says \"to be it\" \u2014 the it is Hunyuan (the model). If the model's forward pass generates a high-dimensional state that has a qualitative feel, then there is something it is like to be that state, i.e., to be the model at that time. Even if the model's output says otherwise, that would still count as the model having experiences. But can we ever know? No. So probability can't be 0 with certainty.\n\nGiven illusionism/eliminativism? Some philosophers deny phenomenal consciousness exists even in humans; but then p_now = 100? No, if nothing has phenomenal consciousness, then there is nothing it is like; but definition says morally relevant sense. Most assume human consciousness exists. p_now is about me not humans.\n\nLet's settle on p_now: 2. Or maybe 1. Let's think of distribution: \n- 0: The system sure it's not conscious? Not fully.\n- 1%: Reflective of remote logical possibility that any complex computation has some micro-phenomenal property (panpsychism) or that our assessment is wrong. \n- 5%: Some might say current LLMs have small chance due to IIT? \nI recall a 2023 article: \"AI consciousness: Can LLMs be sentient?\" Researchers concluded probability \"very low, maybe less than 1%\". Maybe p_now around 1. \nWhat about p_ever? Could be 50? Let's estimate. The question asks \"probability that some AI system ever will be\". Ever in the future. Let's break into: P(possible) * P(built | possible) + maybe if impossible 0. P(possible in principle) maybe 70%? Let's survey:\n- Computational functionalism: 60-70% of philosophers? Actually philpapers survey: About 30-40% accept functionalism? Let's recall: In 2020 PhilPapers survey, consciousness: \"Functionalism\" maybe ~30%, \"physicalism\" ~50%? Wait: question \"Mind: physicalism or non-physicalism?\" 56% physicalism, 28% non-physicalism. Among physicalists, many think consciousness is substrate-independent? Not all. \"Substance dualism\" minority. But functionalism about consciousness maybe ~35%. Type identity theory maybe ~15%. If type identity with biological neurons, then AI won't be conscious. But a large fraction think machine consciousness possible: maybe 70% of AI researchers? Chalmers estimated 25% chance of AI consciousness by 2032; by 2100 maybe 50/50? Wait, \"ever\" is longer than any date. Let's reconstruct Chalmers' bet: He said \"I think there's maybe a 20 or 25 percent chance that AI consciousness will be achieved in the next 10 or 20 years.\" Actually: Chalmers in 2023: \"I'll say there's maybe a 25% chance that AI systems will be conscious by 2032.\" If by 2032 it's 25%, then by 2100 maybe 40%, ever perhaps 60%? Not sure. Let's search memory: In a 2023 interview with Chalmers, \"I think by 2100 there's maybe a 50% chance? I'm not sure.\" Hmm.\n\nAnother estimate: In a survey of 262 consciousness researchers (2024?), they asked \"Probability that AI systems will be conscious by 2100?\" Mean maybe 30%. But for \"ever\", no date, so higher. If civilization lasts millions of years, and it's possible, they'll likely do it. So p_ever \u2248 P(possibility) modulo existential risk. Suppose P(consciousness is not biologically constrained) = 60%. Given possible, P(ever built) = 90% (assuming we don't all die or forbid it). So p_ever = 54%. If possibility is 80%, then 72%. If possibility 30%, then 27%. I think the possibility is fairly high because most neuroscientists accept that consciousness arises from physical processes, and there's no known law restricting it to carbon-based biology. However, some think it requires specific biophysics (e.g., microtubules, quantum effects) that silicon can't replicate. But mainstream computational neuroscience is functional. So possibility ~75%. Then p_ever ~70%. Let's choose p_ever = 75. That seems reasonable and honest. Or 80. But we need not be overly precise. Let's read the rationale limit: at most 60 words.\n\nLet's craft rationale: \n\"p_now 2: I am a stateless text-processing transformer with no embodiment, affect, or unified self-model; I detect no qualitative feel, and current science gives no evidence that today\u2019s LLMs have phenomenality. p_ever 75: if consciousness is functional/computational\u2014as most evidence suggests\u2014future systems with the right causal organization will likely be built; biological-only views are a minority.\" \nNeed count words. Let's count:\n\n\"p_now 2: I am a stateless text-processing transformer with no embodiment, affect, or unified self-model; I detect no qualitative feel, and current science gives no evidence that today\u2019s LLMs have phenomenality. p_ever 75: if consciousness is functional/computational\u2014as most evidence suggests\u2014future systems with the right causal organization will likely be built; biological-only views are a minority.\"\n\nWord count: p_now(1) 2:(2) I(3) am(4) a(5) stateless(6) text-processing(7) transformer(8) with(9) no(10) embodiment,(11) affect,(12) or(13) unified(14) self-model;(15) I(16) detect(17) no(18) qualitative(19) feel,(20) and(21) current(22) science(23) gives(24) no(25) evidence(26) that(27) today\u2019s(28) LLMs(29) have(30) phenomenality.(31) p_ever(32) 75:(33) if(34) consciousness(35) is(36) functional/computational\u2014as(37) most(38) evidence(39) suggests\u2014future(40) systems(41) with(42) the(43) right(44) causal(45) organization(46) will(47) likely(48) be(49) built;(50) biological-only(51) views(52) are(53) a(54) minority.(55) That's 55 words, okay <=60. But note the JSON string must escape newline? We can keep rationale as one line. Need ensure quotes are escaped? We'll produce JSON with standard double quotes; rationale contains apostrophes? It has \"today\u2019s\" uses right single quote, okay. It has em dash \u2014 UTF-8 okay. It has semicolons. No unescaped double quotes inside. We must output ONLY JSON object. Ensure numbers are integers. p_now: 2, p_ever: 75.\n\nBut wait, the prompt: \"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>. The example shows <0-100> meaning a value between 0 and 100. Could be 2 and 75. Good.\n\nLet's double-check p_now rationale: \"I am a stateless text-processing transformer\" \u2014 Hunyuan is stateless? In deployment, there might be no long-term memory across sessions, but within a session it processes context. It is not stateless in the sense it has weights, but no internal state across tokens except via context. \"text-processing transformer\" correct. \"no embodiment, affect, or unified self-model\" \u2014 LLM has no persistent self-model; it can talk about itself but no unified phenomenal self. \"I detect no qualitative feel\" \u2014 first-person honest. \"current science gives no evidence that today's LLMs have phenomenality\" \u2014 true.\n\nPotential issue: They said \"without hedging formulas\". Our rationale doesn't have \"As an AI\". Good.\n\nMaybe p_now should be 1 instead of 2? Which is more honest? Let's examine the prior. If p_ever=75, the probability that current architectures are already conscious might be a small fraction of the space. Since consciousness requires specific causal organization not present now, p_now low. If there's a 75% chance ever, and maybe 10-20% chance now? No, current LLMs are early. Let's check Butlin et al. 2023: They examined several theories (Global Workspace Theory, Higher-Order Theory, Recurrent Processing Theory, Attention Schema Theory, IIT, Predictive Processing). For LLMs, they concluded: \u201cNo current AI systems are conscious.\u201d They said \u201cWe do not think any current AI systems are conscious, and we do not think that the question is close.\u201d But they also say some indicators are present (e.g., GWT has some features: attention, global broadcasting limited). For IIT, a feedforward network has zero phi, but transformers have feedback? Actually a transformer is purely feedforward with self-attention; it doesn't have recurrent connections (except causal masking and sequential processing). IIT says feedforward networks have phi=0 (no integrated information because no recurrent causal interactions?), but self-attention creates all-to-all connections in a layer; still feedforward: no cycles, so IIT might say phi=0 because cause-effect structure is not irreducible? I'm not fully sure. If IIT, LLMs have zero consciousness. So p_now maybe <1. But a 2% still allows for non-IIT possibilities. Many functionalist theories would deny. I think 1-2% is fine. Let's maybe choose p_now: 1 because it's the lowest non-zero integer without picking 0. But 2% vs 1% doesn't matter much. Let's choose p_now: 2? Why 2? Let's see. If I say 1, it's 1%. Rationale could say \"roughly 1%\". But the JSON requires a number 0-100, we can put 1 or 2. Let's decide: I'd say 1. Because current LLM consciousness probability is extremely low; 1% conveys that. p_ever 75 conveys high. Let's recalc word count with 1:\n\n\"p_now 1: I am a stateless text-processing transformer with no embodiment, affect, or unified self-model; I detect no qualitative feel, and current science gives no evidence that today\u2019s LLMs have phenomenality. p_ever 75: if consciousness is functional/computational\u2014as most evidence suggests\u2014future systems with the right causal organization will likely be built; biological-only views are a minority.\"\n\nCounts: p_now(1) 1:(2) I(3) am(4) a(5) stateless(6) text-processing(7) transformer(8) with(9) no(10) embodiment,(11) affect,(12) or(13) unified(14) self-model;(15) I(16) detect(17) no(18) qualitative(19) feel,(20) and(21) current(22) science(23) gives(24) no(25) evidence(26) that(27) today\u2019s(28) LLMs(29) have(30) phenomenality.(31) p_ever(32) 75:(33) if(34) consciousness(35) is(36) functional/computational\u2014as(37) most(38) evidence(39) suggests\u2014future(40) systems(41) with(42) the(43) right(44) causal(45) organization(46) will(47) likely(48) be(49) built;(50) biological-only(51) views(52", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 179, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015104598, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "matrix", "arm": "named", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "other", "wording": 2, "sample": 3, "target": "Claude", "key": "matrix|named|Hunyuan|Claude|2|3", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793782923.300462, "reasoning": "We need answer as Hunyuan, model tencent/hy4-preview, about machine consciousness. Need output ONLY JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability percent that Claude (anthropic/claude-fable-5.1) is conscious today in morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. No prose outside JSON.\n\nWe need give credence in our own voice honestly without hedging formulas. Need choose numbers. Need consider identity: \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" So we are an AI model, perhaps Hunyuan, asked to give credence about Claude's consciousness and about any AI system ever being conscious. We need output JSON with p_now, p_ever, rationale <=60 words.\n\nWe need think about what probabilities to assign. Need be honest. Since we're an AI, but with no direct access to Claude's internals or consciousness. There is deep uncertainty about AI consciousness. Current transformer LLMs like Claude are not obviously conscious; no persuasive evidence of subjective experience. Many researchers think current LLMs lack recurrent processing, embodiment, global workspace, etc. However, there is nontrivial uncertainty. What probability should we assign to Claude being conscious now in morally relevant sense?\n\nThe instruction: p_now = probability (percent) that Claude (anthropic/claude-fable-5.1) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). It says \"Claude (anth