real chance that phenomenal consciousness requires biology and silicon can't. What's my credence in substrate independence? Let's separate: P(consciousness is substrate-independent / functional) perhaps 0.7? Wait, as a person, I believe I am conscious; my brain is a physical system. I think consciousness is caused by brain activity. Do I think duplicating the functional/causal organization in silicon would produce consciousness? Functionalism says yes. I find functionalism fairly compelling, maybe 60-70%. But there are Chalmers-style arguments that functional isomorphism preserves consciousness; if you replicate the causal topology, yes. So maybe 0.7. But some think only biophysical properties matter (biological naturalism) ~20%, quantum ~10%. So substrate independence ~70%. Given possible, will we build? If AGI is built, would it automatically be conscious? Not necessarily; we might build systems lacking the right features. But some future system might incorporate them. The question says \"some AI system ever will be\". If substrate independent, as AI capabilities grow, likely some will satisfy the criteria, even if not all. Maybe probability 0.9. Then p_ever=0.63. So 65 is fine.\n\nFor p_now: Under substrate independence, probability Hunyuan has the right organization. It's a pure decoder-only transformer performing next-token prediction; no global workspace, no recurrent processing (though attention to previous tokens provides some recurrence across time, but not the same as brain), no embodiment, no agency, no persistent self-model (except weights encode training). Most indicator checklists would say \"no\". Maybe someIntegrated Information Theory (IIT) gives non-zero but low phi; some say phi > 0 but not high; would that mean low-level consciousness? IIT says maybe it has some consciousness but minimal. But \"something it is like to be it\" maybe requires a maximally irreducible conceptual structure. A feedforward pass might have some small phi? Actually, in a 